== fuller page ==
A ghostwheel is a set of computers in rings, so that when one is away, the
others carry on. The first design has seventeen of them [hosts]. Fifteen stand
in three rings of five, and a ring is available while any three of its five
are [rings]. The same fifteen also make five rings of three [lesser]. The edge
ring holds all seventeen, in service or not: it is where hosts are added and
taken away, and where the rings are built [edge].

Nobody picked those numbers one by one. Take seventeen, keep two aside (one to
build on, one to test on), and fifteen are left. Divide by three, and each big
ring has five. Turn that over, and the small rings hold three [derive].

What runs on the wheel is C4, published in 2025 as "Continuous Creation,
Command & Control, a literate technology control framework" [public]. Its aim,
in one line: make the thing findable later, by someone who was not there,
without asking them to trust you [findable]. No AI is needed to use it [noai].

Spoke and Pin are this book's two guides. Spoke sees. Pin writes down what
Spoke saw, with when and where. Each small mark on this page opens what Pin
wrote.

