== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Come in. This is the shop, and we're glad you're here.
narrator: A ghostwheel is a lot of small computers, in rings. Each one looks after its friends.
spoke: Hello, you. I'm Spoke. I live at the middle of the wheel, and I see things.
pin: And I'm Pin. I write down what Spoke sees, for you. And when. And where.
[fuller A]
pin [hosts]: Here is the first design. Seventeen computers.
spoke [rings]: Fifteen of them stand in three rings of five. While any three of the five are there, the ring is up. Count them with me.
pin [lesser]: The same fifteen also make five rings of three. Do you see the colours?
spoke [edge]: And the edge ring holds all seventeen, working or not. That's where you would add a computer, or take one away.
pin [derive]: Try it with me. Seventeen. Keep two aside, one to build on and one to test on. That leaves fifteen. Divide by three, and you get rings of five. Turn it over, and you get rings of three.
narrator [public]: What runs on the wheel is called {C4|C four}.
narrator [findable]: It is there so you can find a thing later, even if you weren't there, and no one has to ask you to trust them.
spoke: Every little mark on this page opens what Pin wrote down.
pin: When it was seen, who saw it, and which file it came from. It's all there for you.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: This is the shop. Come in.
narrator: A ghostwheel is a lot of small computers, in rings. Each one looks after its friends.
narrator: Spoke lives at the middle, and sees. Pin writes down what Spoke sees.
spoke: Pin. Someone new, at the door.
pin: I see them. Writing it down.
spoke: Hello, you.
[fuller B]
narrator [hosts]: The first design has seventeen computers.
narrator [rings]: Fifteen stand in three rings of five, and a ring is available while any three of its five are.
narrator [lesser]: The same fifteen also make five rings of three.
spoke [edge]: And the edge, Pin. Don't forget the edge ring. All seventeen, working or not.
pin [edge]: Got it. That's where computers come and go.
narrator [derive]: Nobody picked those numbers one by one. Take seventeen, and keep two aside, one to build on and one to test on. Fifteen are left. Divide by three, and each big ring has five. Turn that over, and the small rings hold three.
narrator [public]: What runs on the wheel is {C4|C four}.
narrator [findable]: Its aim is to make the thing findable later, by someone who was not there, without asking them to trust you.
spoke: Who wrote all that down, Pin?
pin: I did. I always do.
pin: Every little mark on the page opens my notes. Go on, you can look.

